# Target C-II — low-`t` pentagon sea and outer-to-outer train floor

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `45b2e811`  
**Status:** proved proof-only structural consequence; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core Type-II branch, using the proof-only AG42 train gap  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

Let

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
C_0:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\]

and on a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t:=\lim{q-k\over D},\quad
\kappa:=\lim{k\over D},\quad
\delta:=\lim{b-P_F\over D},\quad
\varphi:=\lim{f\over D},\quad
\chi:=\lim{Q\over D}.
\]

The audited §171 identities and the proof-only AG42 input imply

\[
\chi=2t-\pi-\delta,
\tag{PS.1}
\]

\[
C={t\over2}+2\varphi-{\kappa\over2}\ge C_0,
\tag{PS.2}
\]

with `0<=varphi<=chi` because every bounded nonquadrilateral contact face has degree at least five.

These two elementary constraints have a strong low-`t` consequence which was not previously isolated: every low-`t` survivor contains a linear **sea of pentagonal defect faces**, and simultaneously a linear family of outer-to-outer stopped trains.

## 1. Exceptional-face count

From (PS.2),

\[
\boxed{
\varphi\ge {C_0\over2}-{t\over4}+{\kappa\over4}.
}
\tag{PS.3}
\]

Thus even before using the face-degree distribution, low `t` forces about one exceptional face per two units of diameter.

## 2. Almost all excess degree disappears near the present floor

Because

\[
Q-f=\sum_F(\deg F-5),
\]

we have, using (PS.1) and (PS.3),

\[
\boxed{
{Q-f\over D}
\le {9t\over4}-\pi-{C_0\over2}-\delta-{\kappa\over4}+o(1).
}
\tag{PS.4}
\]

Let `f_{>=6}` be the number of bounded exceptional faces of degree at least six. Each such face contributes at least one unit to `Q-f`, so

\[
f_{\ge6}\le Q-f.
\tag{PS.5}
\]

Let `p_5` be the number of pentagonal bounded exceptional faces. Then

\[
p_5=f-f_{\ge6}\ge2f-Q,
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
{p_5\over D}
\ge \pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{PS.6}
\]

This is the main structural conclusion. It does not assume a positive area or Oler gap per pentagon; the known near-degenerate pentagon regression remains fully compatible with the theorem.

## 3. Numerical form at the current proof-only floor

Put

\[
C_{146}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right),
\qquad
t_{42}:=C_{146}+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447\ldots .
\]

At the most permissive values `delta=kappa=0`, equations (PS.3)--(PS.6) give

\[
\boxed{
{f\over D}\ge0.4973006473690961\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{PS.7}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{Q-f\over D}\le0.0028839750000000\ldots+o(1),
}
\tag{PS.8}
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
{p_5\over D}\ge0.4944166723690961\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{PS.9}
\]

In particular the proportion of exceptional faces which can have degree at least six is at most

\[
{0.002883975\over0.4973006473}<0.0058
\]

at the endpoint profile. More than `99.42%` of the exceptional faces are forced to be pentagons there.

The conclusion remains strong throughout the old endpoint/common-cap band. If

\[
t\le1.8585,
\]

then simply dropping `delta,kappa>=0` from (PS.6) gives

\[
\boxed{
{p_5\over D}\ge0.4003882673177079\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{PS.10}
\]

Thus any survivor below the separate `1.8585` barrier must carry at least `0.4003D` actual pentagonal contact faces.

## 4. A simultaneous outer-to-outer train floor

Let

- `L_OO` be the number of stopped trains whose two terminal occurrences lie on the outer face,
- `L_OB` the number with one outer and one bounded-face terminal occurrence, and
- `L_BB` the number with two bounded-face terminal occurrences.

The occurrence-counted endpoint ledgers are

\[
b=2L_{OO}+L_{OB},
\tag{PS.11}
\]

\[
Q+4f=L_{OB}+2L_{BB}.
\tag{PS.12}
\]

Hence

\[
L_{OO}-L_{BB}={b-(Q+4f)\over2},
\]

so, since `L_BB>=0`,

\[
L_{OO}\ge {b-(Q+4f)\over2}.
\tag{PS.13}
\]

Audited §171 gives

\[
{Q+4f\over D}=t+2C+\kappa-\pi-\delta
\]

and

\[
C\le {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2}.
\]

Therefore

\[
{Q+4f\over D}\le10t-5\pi-5\delta,
\]

while `b/D=pi+delta`. Substituting into (PS.13) yields

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {L_{OO}\over D}\ge3\pi-5t.
}
\tag{PS.14}
\]

At `t=t_42`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {L_{OO}\over D}\ge0.3203347708721561\ldots .
}
\tag{PS.15}
\]

Throughout the band `t<=1.8585`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {L_{OO}\over D}\ge0.1322779607693797\ldots .
}
\tag{PS.16}
\]

This is significant because audited §157 proves that every outer-to-outer train is crossed by actual diameter edges with both transverse signs. The low-`t` branch therefore contains simultaneously:

1. a linear sea of actual pentagonal defect faces, and
2. a linear family of two-sided diameter-braced outer-to-outer train cuts.

## 5. Frontier effect

This does **not** close C-II. The known pentagon regression shows that a convex unit pentagon can approach all three triangular-lattice projective directions while its extra diagonals approach unit length, so no uniform per-face angular or Oler penalty is available.

The useful narrowing is global. Any attempt to keep `t` below `1.8585` must now realize a contact complex with at least `0.400388D-o(D)` pentagonal bounded faces and at least `0.132278D-o(D)` outer-to-outer trains, each of which is two-sidedly braced by the actual diameter graph. Near the present `t_42` floor these populations strengthen to approximately `0.494417D` pentagons and `0.320335D` outer-to-outer trains.

The next plausible closure target is therefore a **global compatibility theorem** between the near-lattice pentagon network and the convex-diameter thrackle / nested outer-train cut system. Local pentagon nondegeneracy alone is false.

## 6. Reproducibility

The arithmetic certificate is `research/verify_target_c_low_t_pentagon_sea.py`.
